[38;5;252m[1m## What the library is[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;10mc_src[0m is a single translation unit exporting exactly one symbol, [38;5;10mmerge_sort[0m — a two-buffer merge sort over [38;5;10mspritebatch_sprite_t { u64 texture_id; int sort_bits; }[0m. The other three functions are [38;5;10mstatic[0m and have no dynamic symbol. No enums, no flags, no [38;5;10m#ifdef[0m, no error returns; [38;5;10mmerge_sort[0m returns [38;5;10mvoid[0m.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;252m[1m## The divergence[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The original Rust formed [38;5;10m&*ptr[0m references to sprites and used [38;5;10mptr::copy_nonoverlapping[0m. Those carry alignment, non-overlap, and non-null preconditions [1mthat the C does not have[22m — the C just emits [38;5;10mmov[0ms and plain address arithmetic (confirmed against the disassembly). Under the dev profile's UB checks, four tests aborted:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;10mmisaligned pointer dereference: address must be a multiple of 0x8
unsafe precondition(s) violated: ptr::copy_nonoverlapping requires that both
pointer arguments are aligned and non-null and the ranges do not overlap
[0m[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The translation was relying on latent UB that only happened to work with optimizations on. Rewrote [38;5;10msrc/lib.rs[0m to mirror gcc's codegen: [38;5;10mread_unaligned[0m field loads instead of references, a two-[38;5;10mu64[0m load-then-store sprite copy (matching gcc's exact instruction sequence, padding included), [38;5;10mwrapping_offset[0m instead of [38;5;10moffset[0m, and a direct libc [38;5;10mmemcpy[0m call for the bulk copy — which also makes the near-[38;5;10m2^64[0m length from a negative [38;5;10msize[0m hit literally the same code as the C.[0m[0m
